From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cbm <omarchy-mobile@kloot.lu>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 19:07:27 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] drm/panel: google-tg4c: Accept the TG4C build that reads ID3
 0x40

Commit 77ea6afd9f55 ("drm/panel: google-tg4c: Send no commands to a panel that is not a TG4C")
takes a panel whose ID3 is not 0x00 for another panel and sends it
nothing. A second Pixel 9a reads ID 04 80 40 2d, and its bootloader
passes exynos_drm.panel_name=google-tg4c.2d408004 -- the same four
bytes, so ABL itself identifies this panel as a TG4C. With the check
failing, every backlight write and every 60/120 Hz switch on that phone
returned -ENODEV:

  panel-google-tg4c 19440000.dsi.0: panel ID 04 80 40 2d is not a TG4C's:
  sending it no commands
  gs-dsim-cmd 19440000.dsi: switching from 60 to 120 Hz failed: -19

Accept ID3 0x40 as well. ID1 is still checked and ID3 must be one of
the two values seen on TG4C panels, so any other ID still gets no
commands. Google's panel-gs-tg4c.c treats the two builds the same: its
tg4c_get_panel_rev() picks the command revisions from the build code in
0xDB alone, and that byte is 0x80 on both panels, so Google's own driver
sends this panel exactly the TG4C commands.

Tested on a Pixel 9a (tegu, panel ID 04 80 40 2d) with
7.3.0-rc6-omamobile-push1-ge8d722982099, whose panel-google-tg4c.c is
this commit's (blob 8901b8f50009):

- the "not a TG4C" message is gone, and writing 600 and then 3000 to
  /sys/class/backlight/19440000.dsi.0/brightness succeeds instead of
  failing with -ENODEV;
- the refresh rate, switched from Hyprland 120 -> 60 -> 120 -> 60 ->
  120 Hz: gs-decon logs each switch with no error, and with a terminal
  redrawing every frame the DECON frame-start interrupt counts 120, 60,
  121, 60 and 121 a second;
- the display off and on (DPMS) at 60 Hz and at 120 Hz: the frames come
  back at the same rate and the next brightness write succeeds;
- LHBM at 120 Hz with allow_lhbm=1: local_hbm_mode reads 1 after
  writing 1 and 0 after writing 0, with no error, and a switch to 60 Hz
  ends it (the circle itself was not looked at).

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: cbm <omarchy-mobile@kloot.lu>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-google-tg4c.c | 10 +++++++---
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-google-tg4c.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-google-tg4c.c
index b42431301..8901b8f50 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-google-tg4c.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-google-tg4c.c
@@ -58,12 +58,16 @@ static const u8 tg4c_cmd2_page2[] = { 0xF0, 0x55, 0xAA, 0x52, 0x08, 0x02 };
  * The panel's ID as Google's gs_panel reads it: MIPI DCS ID1 (the module's
  * manufacturer), ID2 (its version), ID3 (the module/driver ID), and the first
  * byte of the device descriptor block. The bootloader appends the same four
- * bytes, last first, to exynos_drm.panel_name: google-tg4c.2d008004 on the
- * Pixel 9a's TG4C, so ID1 0x04 and ID3 0x00.
+ * bytes, last first, to exynos_drm.panel_name. Pixel 9a TG4Cs come in two
+ * builds: google-tg4c.2d008004 (ID3 0x00) and google-tg4c.2d408004 (ID3
+ * 0x40). Both read ID1 0x04 and ID2 0x80, the build code Google's
+ * panel-gs-tg4c.c picks its command revisions from, so both get the same
+ * commands.
  */
 static const u8 tg4c_id_regs[] = { 0xda, 0xdb, 0xdc, MIPI_DCS_READ_DDB_START };
 #define TG4C_ID1		0x04
 #define TG4C_ID3		0x00
+#define TG4C_ID3_ALT		0x40
 
 enum tg4c_id {
 	TG4C_ID_UNREAD,
@@ -127,7 +131,7 @@ static bool tg4c_check_id(struct tg4c *ctx)
 		}
 	}
 
-	if (id[0] != TG4C_ID1 || id[2] != TG4C_ID3) {
+	if (id[0] != TG4C_ID1 || (id[2] != TG4C_ID3 && id[2] != TG4C_ID3_ALT)) {
 		dev_err(dev, "panel ID %*ph is not a TG4C's: sending it no commands\n",
 			(int)sizeof(id), id);
 		ctx->id = TG4C_ID_OTHER;
